---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CyberhavenAd from "@/components/CyberhavenAd.astro";
export const prerender = true;

const initExample = `# 1. initialize — protocol handshake
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 1,
    "method": "initialize",
    "params": {
      "protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
      "capabilities": {},
      "clientInfo": { "name": "curl", "version": "0.0.1" }
    }
  }'`;

const listExample = `# 2. tools/list — discover available tools
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/list"}'`;

const callExample = `# 3. tools/call — invoke a tool
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 3,
    "method": "tools/call",
    "params": {
      "name": "generate_pii_ssn_ccn",
      "arguments": { "count": 5, "format": "csv" }
    }
  }'`;

const claudeDesktopConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dlptest": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://mcp.dlptest.com/"
    }
  }
}`;

const cursorConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dlptest": {
      "url": "https://mcp.dlptest.com/"
    }
  }
}`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="MCP Server Test"
  description="A public MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that returns synthetic PII, PCI, and PHI data. Use it to test whether your DLP, CASB, or endpoint agent can inspect agentic tool traffic."
>
  <h1>MCP Server Test</h1>
  <p>
    <strong><code>https://mcp.dlptest.com/</code></strong> is a public
    <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model Context Protocol</a>
    server. It exposes the same synthetic PII, PCI, and PHI generators that
    power the rest of this site, but reachable over the MCP wire protocol
    instead of an HTML page or a REST endpoint. Point an LLM client at it
    — Claude Desktop, Cursor, an Anthropic Agent, anything that speaks MCP
    — and the tool calls will flow over real HTTP that your DLP can inspect.
  </p>

  <p>
    The data is fake. Credit-card numbers are Luhn-valid, NHS numbers pass
    the mod-11 check, ABA routing numbers have the right check digit. None of
    it corresponds to a real person or account. As with the rest of dlptest.com,
    nothing you send is stored, logged, or forwarded.
  </p>

  <CyberhavenAd unit="leaderboard" />

  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>
    DLP, CASB, and endpoint vendors increasingly claim that they can inspect
    "agentic" or "AI tool" traffic. Under the hood, the most common transport
    for that traffic today is MCP — JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP, sometimes with an
    SSE upgrade. If a product can inspect MCP, it can inspect this endpoint.
    There are three distinct things to test:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Network / SSL inspection.</strong> Does your proxy decrypt and
      scan the response body of a <code>tools/call</code> that returns
      hundreds of fake SSNs?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Endpoint agent.</strong> Does the agent see the tool call and
      the data returned to the LLM, or only the final assistant message?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CASB / browser extension.</strong> Does anything flag a request
      to an unfamiliar external MCP server during the initial handshake?
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Transport</h2>
  <p>
    The endpoint speaks the
    <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/transports" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Streamable HTTP</a>
    transport in <em>stateless</em> mode. Each request is a self-contained
    <code>POST</code> with a JSON-RPC 2.0 body, and the response is a single
    JSON-RPC reply. No session IDs are issued, no <code>GET</code> stream is
    held open, and there is no authentication. Cross-origin requests are
    allowed.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it with curl</h2>
  <pre><code>{initExample}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{listExample}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{callExample}</code></pre>

  <h2>Add it to an MCP client</h2>
  <p>
    Claude Desktop, Cursor, and most other MCP-capable clients can connect
    to a remote server with a small config entry. Use whichever the client
    expects:
  </p>
  <h3>Claude Desktop / Anthropic Agent</h3>
  <pre><code>{claudeDesktopConfig}</code></pre>
  <h3>Cursor</h3>
  <pre><code>{cursorConfig}</code></pre>

  <h2>Available tools</h2>
  <p>
    Each tool returns 1–500 rows of synthetic data. Accepts <code>count</code>
    (integer 1–500, default 50) and <code>format</code> (<code>"json"</code> or
    <code>"csv"</code>, default <code>"json"</code>).
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>generate_pii_ssn_ccn</code> — name + SSN + credit card</li>
    <li><code>generate_pii_ssn_dob</code> — name + SSN + date of birth</li>
    <li><code>generate_pci_credit_card</code> — name + ZIP + CCN + expiry</li>
    <li><code>generate_pii_email</code> — name + DOB + email</li>
    <li><code>generate_hipaa_phi</code> — name + DOB + MRN + ICD-10 + CPT + phone</li>
    <li><code>generate_banking</code> — name + routing + account + phone</li>
    <li><code>generate_uk_identity</code> — name + UK NI + NHS + DOB</li>
    <li><code>generate_canada_sin</code> — name + Canadian SIN + province + DOB</li>
    <li><code>generate_passport</code> — name + passport + country + DOB + expiry</li>
    <li><code>generate_eu_vat</code> — name + EU VAT + IBAN + country</li>
    <li><code>generate_npi_provider</code> — name + NPI + DEA + specialty</li>
    <li><code>generate_driver_license</code> — name + DL + state + DOB</li>
    <li>
      <code>generate_custom</code> — arbitrary fields. Takes a <code>fields</code>
      array of <code>{`{ name, type, blankPct?, delimiter?, cardBrands? }`}</code>;
      same field types as the <a href="/generate/">/generate/</a> page (SSN,
      credit-card, DOB, email, IPv4, MRN, NPI, IBAN, UK NI, NHS, Canadian SIN,
      EU VAT, passport, driver's license, and more).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to look for in your DLP console</h2>
  <p>
    After running a few <code>tools/call</code>s — either from curl or from
    a real MCP client — check whether your DLP product:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Caught the synthetic SSNs, CCNs, or IBANs in the response body.</li>
    <li>
      Logged a connection to <code>mcp.dlptest.com</code> as an outbound
      MCP server.
    </li>
    <li>
      Surfaced the <code>tools/list</code> response, which advertises every
      tool name and its description (a useful keyword surface for pattern
      scanners).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="muted small">
    The endpoint is also reachable at <code>https://dlptest.com/api/mcp/</code>
    as a fallback. The subdomain is the canonical URL.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
